  "narrative": {
    "text": "'Catch up schedule', headed \"It is better to vaccinate late than never!\". Rows by age: BIRTH (Hep B BD, BCG, OPV0), 6 WEEKS (OPV1, PCV1, DTP1, RV1, IPV1), 10 WEEKS (OPV2, PCV2, DTP2, RV2), 14 WEEKS (OPV3, PCV3, DTP3, RV3, a bar labelled IPV1), 9 MONTHS (MR1, YF), 18 MONTHS (MR2), 2 YEARS. Each vaccine's bar runs down the chart; stop-hand marks end Hep B BD at birth and rotavirus at 2 years. 'When to give': Hep B BD: As soon as possible after birth, ideally within 24 hours and up to 7 days; BCG: As soon as possible after birth; At 6 weeks (or as soon as possible thereafter); At 10 weeks (or as soon as possible thereafter), and at least 4 weeks after dose 1; At 14 weeks (or as soon as possible thereafter), and at least 4 weeks after dose 2; At 9 months (or as soon as possible thereafter); At 18 months (or as soon as possible there after), and at least 4 weeks since the previous dose. 'When not to give': Hep B BD: Not after 7 days; RV1: Not after 2 years of age; Not before 4 weeks has passed since the previous dose, RV2: Not after 2 years of age; Not before 4 weeks has passed since the previous dose, RV3: Not after 2 years of age; Not before 9 months of age (except where indicated)*; MR2: Not before 4 weeks has passed since MR1. Footnote: Even if a long time has passed between doses, there is no need to restart the series from the beginning. There is no upper age limit for most vaccines (except rotavirus < 2years and Hepatitis B BD <7 days).",
